      article.main
        h1 Case
        p
          | The
          code case
          | statement is a shorthand for JavaScript’s
          code switch
          | statement. It takes the following form:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="0")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-382428418")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var friends = 10
                  case friends
                    when 0
                      p you have no friends
                    when 1
                      p you have a friend
                    default
                      p you have #{friends} friends
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <p>you have 10 friends</p>
        h2#case-fall-through
          | Case Fall Through
          a.header-anchor(href="#case-fall-through", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | You can use fall through, just as you would in a JavaScript
          code switch
          | statement.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="1")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1496684683")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var friends = 0
                  case friends
                    when 0
                    when 1
                      p you have very few friends
                    default
                      p you have #{friends} friends
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <p>you have very few friends</p>
        p
          | The difference, however, is a fall through in JavaScript happens whenever a
          code break
          | statement is not explicitly included; in Pug, it only happens when a block is completely missing.
        p
          | If you would like to not output anything in a specific case, add an explicit unbuffered
          code break
          | :
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="2")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-831747094")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var friends = 0
                  case friends
                    when 0
                      - break
                    when 1
                      p you have very few friends
                    default
                      p you have #{friends} friends
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7")
        h2#block-expansion
          | Block Expansion
          a.header-anchor(href="#block-expansion", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p Block expansion may also be used:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="3")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1358600651")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var friends = 1
                  case friends
                    when 0: p you have no friends
                    when 1: p you have a friend
                    default: p you have #{friends} friends
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <p>you have a friend</p>
        div(style="margin-top: 50px")
